.B holypkg db transactions reports every transaction this root committed. A commit
leaves a directory named for the plan it committed with, a committed file
naming the same plan, and the decisions it was reviewed with: the journal a
set used, the one line a removal was approved with, and the journal a
replacement committed with. Three shapes are recognised, a set, a removal
and a replacement, and a directory that is none of them is not a completed
transaction, so it keeps blocking other work rather than being ignored.

transaction IDENTITY kind KIND GENERATION [FACTS] decisions COUNT
transaction-decision IDENTITY LINE

The record a set keeps is its journal without the phase line, since that line
is where the transaction stopped rather than a decision it was reviewed with.
Every decision line is printed as the record holds it, so this command
restates nothing: artifact, binding, catalog-index, accept-arch,
accept-privileged, skip-hooks, service, private and accept-broken. A search
decision is kept as the plan settled it, naming the file whose search path
changes, the library it names, the artifact that owns the private copy and
the directory it moved to, so a recovery repeats that rewrite instead of
resolving the decision again against a root that has already changed. The facts are the
generation the transaction was reviewed at, the artifact count of a set or
the artifact of a removal, and the old and new artifact of a replacement.
Transactions are in identity order. --json uses the holy-transactions-1
schema with one object per transaction and a summary object. A root without
a usable database is 6.

